## 1. Introduction
Partial hydrogenations of organic substrates such as $\alpha,\ \beta\cdot$ - unsaturated aldehydes are vital chemical transformations that have attracted remarkable attention due to their rich oxygen contents [1–5]. a, ${\mathfrak{\beta}}\qquad$ -unsaturated aldehydes (UAL), for instance, Cinnamaldehydes (CAL) and Furfural (FFL), have two competing unsaturated sites, i.e., olefins bond $({\mathsf{C}}{=}{\mathsf{C}})$ and carbonyls bond $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{C}=0)$ bond, which leads to a complicated multipath reaction as shown in scheme 1. Thermodynamically and kinetically, the partial hydrogenation of the olefinic bond $\scriptstyle(={\mathsf{C}})$ is more favorable than the carbonyls bond $({\mathsf{C}}{=}0)$ . Hence, it is a conundrum from the scientific perspective and highly desired to improve the selectivity of the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond, which leads to fabricating unsaturated alcohols (UOL). UOL can be extensively employed as important intermediates in the production of numerous valuable fine chemicals like perfumes, pharmaceuticals, food additives [4–7]. Moreover, UOL, produced from the reduction of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond, is an essential chemical intermediate in polymer industry, mainly used for the production of thermostatic resins, liquid resins for strengthening ceramics, and in the fabrication of various synthetic fibers, farm chemicals, and rubbers–resins. In addition, as chemical intermediate, it is used for the manufacture of dispersing agents, lubricants, lysine, vitamin C, and plasticizers [8–10]. Many efforts and strategies have been devoted to improving the selectivity toward the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond hydrogenation, such as using bimetallic (IMNs) catalysts with specific geometric and electronic structure, introducing promotors, regulating the metal nanoparticles size, utilizing active support, and so on [1–5,7]. Thus, for the high yield of unsaturated alcohols through the hydrogenation of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond, the establishment of effective catalysts with high activity, selectivity, and marvelous stability is always the scientific community’s desire.
Noble metals are extensively utilized as active catalysts for the hydrogenation of $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated aldehydes (UAL), nonetheless, they still suffer high costs and poor selectivity towards the desired product. The $\mathrm{Pt}.$ -based catalysts are broadly and well-investigated due to high activity and selectivity towards the desired UOL [6,11–14]. To further enhance the selectivity towards UOL and to lessen the use of Pt metal, different electropositive non-noble metals (second metals/promoters) are used as promoters to tune the surface electronic properties, which can regulate the selectivity towards UOL by altering the adsorption/desorption properties of the corresponding substrates [6,12,14–18]. The electronic structure of the first active Pt metal is modulated by the second electropositive or heteroatom metals due to electronic or synergistic effects [2,3,6,14], which further generates electrophilic and nucleophilic sites. These electrophilic and nucleophilic sites have been proven to be responsible for enhanced activity and selectivity [2,14,19]. Despite profound achievements, the fabrication of distinctive stabilized Pt-based catalysts rich in electrophilic and nucleophilic sites with remarkable efficiency and selectivity towards UOL is still highly desirable.
Numerous active host materials have been employed to stabilize the noble metal nanoparticles and enhance the catalytic performance through strong metal-support interaction (SMSI) [3,4,11–13,20,21]. Numerous supports such as metal oxides $\mathrm{\TiO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}–\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , etc.), carbon materials, and silica are used for the hydrogenation of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond [3,4,11–13,20,21]. Recently, Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) have attracted remarkable attention due to their highly crystalline porous structure. MOFs, also called porous coordination polymers-PCP, have a high surface area, well-defined 3D or 2D crystalline structure, tunable porosity, and low density [6,15,18,22]. MOFs have composed of metal ions connected by organic ligands, fabricating welldefined zeo-type structures. Meanwhile, confinement or encapsulation of metal nanoparticles within the micro and mesopores of MOFs can reduce the probability of agglomeration and detachment, improving the half-life and durability of noble metal catalysts [23–26]. The pore channels of MOFs induce the steric and geometric effects that immensely improve its applications in selective transformations. Moreover, MOFs’ well-defined microenvironment influences activity and particularly tunes the desired product’s selectivity [5,6,15,23].
MOFs have prospective application in selective hydrogenation reactions, and due to the presence of unsaturated metal ions that act as Lewis acid sites (electrophilic sites), the adsorption, as well as selectivity of the desired carbonyls $({\mathsf{C}}{=}0)$ bond, can be extremely enhanced [15,16,26]. Compared to other MOFs, the MIL-101 (Cr) have high chemical and thermal stability [16,24]. Interestingly, the metal nanoparticles incorporated into MIL-101(Cr) or functionalized MIL-101(Cr) demonstrated high activity, selectivity, and durability in the catalytic reactions [16,22,26,27]. It has been proven that the free amine $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ functionalized MOF e.g., MIL-101 $(\mathsf{C r}){\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ , the supported catalyst, has high catalytic performances in the selective hydrogenation of $\mathfrak{a}$ $\upbeta$ -unsaturated aldehydes [22]. The free amine group $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ plays a crucial role in the improved uniform dispersion of metal nanoparticles and the stability of the catalysts against aggregation and oxidation of metal nanoparticles [22,28,32]. The amines group could provide the coordination sites for the active metal species. The nitrogen heteroatom of the free amine $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ group can collaborate with the precious active metals to enhance the electronic interaction and accelerate the electron sharing (transfer) in the catalytic reaction. Hence, the noble metal-nitrogen catalyst has developed as it may be an encouraging catalyst for hydrogenation reactions [29–31]. The nitrogen heteroatom of the amines group $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ interacts with active metal nanoparticles via its lone pair of electrons to modify the electronic structure of the d-orbital of active noble metal nanoparticles. As a result, new interfacial electrophilic and nucleophilic active sites were generated. These interfacial electrophilic and nucleophilic active sites of catalysts sterically hindered the adsorption of $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond while enhancing the adsorption and activation of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond enhanced over these active sites to achieve the desired selectivity towards UOL [14,22,28,32]. Because of the distinctive structural topographies of MOFs, the catalyst integrated of metal nanoparticles with amine-functionalized MOF will be highly favorable for improving both activity and selectivity of an appropriate hydrogenation reaction.
Herein, aiming at the above findings and facts, we report the free amine $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ functionalized MOF $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathbf{x}))$ as active and promising support to confine Pt-based catalysts. Mesoporous MOF- ${\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ support facilitates the formation of uniformly dispersed and stable active Pt-based monometallic catalysts via polyol reduction method. The influence of the aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\cdot\mathsf{B D C})$ precursors modified MOFs $(\mathsf{M O F-N H_{2}(x)};$ supports on the bulk structure, electronic properties and catalytic performance of the Pt-based catalysts were thoroughly explored.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Catalyst synthesis
## 2.1.1. Preparation of $M O F–N H_{2}$
Amine functionalized MOFs $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were synthesized according to the reported literature [32]. Concisely, Cr $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ ( $\cdot4{\mathrm{~mmol}},1.6{\mathrm{~g}}$ , 2-Amino terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ (4 mmol, $\mathbf{0.72\g}^{\cdot}$ ), Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) $(0.32\ \mathrm{g})$ , and DeIonized water $(30~\mathrm{mL}$ were mixed in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ beaker. The mixture was sonicated at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in an open atmosphere. Subsequently, the reaction mixture was moved into a stainless-steel autoclave with Teflon inner layer $(50~\mathrm{\mL})$ and placed in an oven at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ without stirring. Later on, the final yellowishgreen solids were cooled naturally to room temperature and centrifuged at $9500~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $6~\mathrm{{min}}$ to isolate yellowish-green solids. Subsequently, the obtained green solids were purified solvothermaly once in hot N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) $(12\ \mathrm{h})$ , and thrice in hot ethanol $(12\ \mathrm{~h~})$ to remove non-reacted organic ligands inside the pores. Finally, the obtained yellowish-green powders were isolated by centrifugation and labelled as MOF$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ . Dry the obtained solid at $80~^{\circ}C$ overnight and heat-treated directly afterward at $110~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under vacuum to remove the water inside the pores and terminally connect to Cr metal of MOF- ${\cdot}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ . To prepare a series of MOF- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ support, following the above method by differentiating the ratios (quantity) of 2- Amino terephthalic acid $\mathbf{\widetilde{X}}=0.5$ , 1, 1.5, 2, 3 & 4) while keeping the $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ content constant.
## 2.1.2. Preparation of MOF- ${\cdot}N H_{2}(x)$ supported $P t$ -based catalysts
Pt encaged within the pores of MOF- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ catalysts were prepared by a facile and eco-friendly polyol (Ethylene Glycol-EG) reduction method according to previously reported literature [26]. In brief, MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ $(0.15\ {\mathrm{g}})$ was placed into the mixture of $10~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{EG}+5\ \mathrm{mL}$ DMF (A) and agitated for 5 min. Similarly, the $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $(3~\mathrm{\wt\%})$ aqueous solution was mixed with $10\mathrm{mL}$ EG solution (B). Subsequently, the mixture (B) was gradually added to (A) at room temperature for about $20{\mathrm{~min}}$ while constantly stirring and further agitated for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ at the same temperature. Afterward, the mixture was introduced into an ultrasonic bath for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ to make a homogenous mixture and ensure the complete insertion of the $\mathsf{P t}^{4+}$ within the apertures of MOF$\ensuremath{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ . The mixture was then transferred to a stainless steel autoclave using Teflon inner layer and heated at 150C for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ under static conditions. Lastly, the designed $\mathsf{P t}$ -based catalyst was then cooled to room temperature. The final product was isolated and washed with ethanol thoroughly until free of cl- and other impurities, which was verified with an ${\sf A g N O}_{3}$ aqueous solution. To prepare the $3–\mathrm{Pt/MOF–NH_{2}(x)}$ , where $\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\times~}~}=0.5$ , 1.5, 2, 3, & 4, the above preparation method was repeated, and denoted as $3{\cdot}\mathsf{P t}/$ MOF- ${\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ .
## 2.2. Characterization
An inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) was used to measure the contents of platinum in all the prepared catalysts that were accomplished using a PerkinElmer Optima 7000DV analyser. The elemental composition was also determined by Energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) analysis using EDX-JSM5910, JEOL-Japan, analyser. The $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction-XRD analysis was conducted on a Bruker D8 Advance $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffractometer using Cu $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\P}$ $\langle\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) radiation $(40\mathrm{kV},40\mathrm{mA}$ ). FTIR spectra of all the samples were measured with the KBr pellet method using a German BRUKER’s Equinox 55 IR spectrometer. The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms were used at $-196~^{\circ}C$ with a Micromeritics Tristar II surface area and porosity analysis. The catalysts’ pore size and surface area were measured from desorption isotherms by the Barrett-Joiner-Halenda (BJH) and Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) methods. The surface morphology and structure of all the catalysts were evaluated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Hitachi S-4800) and Transmission electron microscope (TEM) (JEM-2100). The surface electronic structure was investigated using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy-XPS (Escalab 250Xi). The surface Lewis acid sites were confirmed by ammonia temperature-programed desorption $\mathrm{\DeltaNH}_{3}$ -TPD) using AutoChem TP5080 chemisorptions analyzer (Xianquan Tianjin china) with a TCD detector, and the signals of ammonia $(m/z=17)$ ) were documented by mass spectra (QIC-20, Hidden).
## 2.3. Liquid-phase selective hydrogenation reaction
The liquid-phase selective hydrogenation reaction of UAL (CAL/ FFL) was carried out in a stainless-steel autoclave ( $\mathrm{\cdot100mL}$ . Briefly, for every hydrogenation reaction, isopropanol as solvent $(15~\mathrm{mL})$ , CAL/FFL $(0.252\ {\mathrm{g}})$ , and catalyst $\left(20.0~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ were put into a vessel (stainless steel autoclave) equipped with a temperaturecontrolled furnace. Next, the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ gas with $0.5\mathrm{MPa}$ was inserted into the reactor to substitute the air three times, and then the $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ gas with 0.5 MPa was introduced to replace the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ gas into the reactor three times. Afterward, the vessel was filled with the particular $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ pressure $(0.6-1.2\mathrm{\MPa})\$ and placed at the required temperature $(30-80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ under constant magnetic stirring $(800~\mathrm{rpm})$ for the specified reaction time ( $\mathrm{10-120~min}^{\cdot}$ ). For the recycling experiments, the used-catalyst was isolated and recovered by centrifugation, washed 3-times with isopropanol, and placed in a vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The dried isolated catalyst can be reused for the next round under the same reaction conditions without any additional treatment. Once the reaction was over, the reaction mixture was centrifuged to isolate the catalyst, and the final products were identified by gas-chromatography (BFRL SP-3420, Beijing, Chain) furnished with flame ionization detection-FID along with an HP-5 capillary column $(30\mathrm{m}\times0.32\mathrm{mm}\times0.25\mathrm{\mum})$ using octanol as the external standard.
## 3. Results and discussions
## 3.1. Catalysts characterization
## 3.1.1. Metal loading quantitation
The existence of the confined Pt nanoparticles within the mesopores of MOF- ${\bf\cdot N H}_{2}({\bf x})$ , where $\mathrm{~\emph~{~x~}~}=0.5$ , 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4, was initially confirmed by ICP-OES. It is shown in Table 1 (first column) that the actual loading of Pt metal achieved was similar in all catalysts, slightly lower than the target $(3\%)$ . Besides, the elemental analysis of $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF–NH}_{2}(2)$ catalyst was executed thru EDX technique and is presented in Fig. S11, where the amount of Pt (weight $\%$ ) determined by ICP-OES (Table 1) was also approved by EDX analysis in Fig. S11 (inset).
## 3.1.2. Bulk-Structure characterizations
The XRD patterns of the as-synthesized original MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ and $3–\mathrm{Pt/MOF–NH_{2}(x)}$ catalysts $\mathbf{\widetilde{X}}=0.5$ , 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4) are shown in Fig. 1 and Fig. S1 All samples exhibit the identical diffraction peaks, and no apparent structural variations were noticed, a statement in an excellent concurrence with the previously reported results of MOF- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ [32,33]. Specifically, two intense peaks are visible in all cases at $2\uptheta\approx8.8^{\circ}$ and $16.3^{\circ}\mathrm{\Omega}$ . These peaks prove that the crystal-structure of the virgin MOF-NH2(x) remains intact and reserved upon changing the ratios of 2-aminoterephthalic acid and loading the Pt nanoparticles. On the other hand, the intensity of the diffraction peaks appeared at $2\ensuremath{\uptheta}\approx8.8\ensuremath{\uprho}$ , and $16.3^{\circ}$ suppressed to a minute extent after the modification of the concentration of 2- aminotherephthalic acid, specifying a minor reduction in the mesoporous ordering and crystallinity of the MOF- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ support [26]. Besides, noticeable minor diffraction peaks (at $2\uptheta\approx40^{\circ},$ ), assignable to the Pt (111) crystal plane (JCPDS no: 04–0802) [19,20], are noticed in the $3–\mathrm{Pt/MOF–NH_{2}(x)}$ catalysts (Fig. 1), signifying that the crystalline Pt nanoparticles are fairly small and evenly dispersed within the mesopores of MOF- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}({\bf x})$ supports. Furthermore, the crystallite size of $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles in 3-Pt/MOF$\ensuremath{\operatorname{NH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ catalysts $\mathbf{\zeta}_{\mathbf{X}}=0.5$ , 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4) was also calculated from the Pt (111) crystal planes using the Scherrer equation (shown in Table 1), recommending that the moderate amount of amines precursors $\mathrm{\langleNH_{2}B D C\rangle}$ are favorable to develop smaller and more evenly confined Pt nanoparticles within the mesopores of aminefunctionalized MOFs.
The Bulk structure of all the pristine MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ and $3{\cdot}\mathrm{Pt}{}/$ MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ were further examined by FTIR spectroscopy, and the corresponding spectra were recorded in the range of $400{-}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ as shown in Fig. 2 and Fig. S2. All the pristine and Pt-supported MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ catalysts possess similar
FTIR-spectra. Correspondingly, the major absorption bands are seen at 3469 and $3382~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for all the catalysts, credited to the primary amines group’s $\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ asymmetric and symmetric vibrations [22,32,34,44]. The peak that appeared at $1654~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to the $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ bending vibrations of the primary amino group [32,34]. Another band that appeared at $1380~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was assigned to the stretching vibrations of the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ bond of the aromatic ring [31]. Besides, the small bands that appeared at 2930 and $2869~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations [32]. An additional narrow and weak bands were spotted at 769, 1099, and $1160~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , credited to d $({\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-H}})$ and (two) ${\boldsymbol{\gamma}}({\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-H}})$ vibrations of the benzene rings respectively. The weak band at $600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be ascribed to the inplane and out-of-plane bending modes of the $C00^{--}$ groups [27]. The existence of all key vibrational bands for the free aminefunctionalized organic linkers in our MOFs approves that the host MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ structure is remained intact after changing the ratios of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ and loading of the Pt nanoparticles, in excellent agreement with the XRD results (Fig. 1).
## 3.1.3. Pore structure analysis
$\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherm measurements evaluated the pore volumes and specific surface areas of the as-synthesized catalysts. The ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms of all the pristine MOF- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ show a sharp uptake under low pressure $(\mathsf{P}/$ $\mathrm{P_{o}}=0.6\mathrm{-}1.0\rangle$ and exhibit a type-IV isotherm (Fig. S3), approving to the IUPAC classification, characteristic of materials comprising mesopores (mesoporous materials) [34]. The BET specific surface area and total pore volume (Table S1) of the virgin MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(1)$ were assessed through the BJH method to be 1909 $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1.31~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. These parameters decline upon changing 2-Aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ concentration and decrease further with decreasing or increasing $\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ concentration, as shown in Table S1. The substantial decrease in these physical parameters values is possibly due to a modified amount of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ . In the same way, all the $\mathrm{Pt}.$ -supported MOF- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ catalysts $(3{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Pt/MOF–NH_{2}(x)})$ catalysts retain the type-IV curve (Fig. 3A), signifying that the host mesoporous structure is well-retained. Besides, as shown in Fig. 3B, all the $3–\mathrm{Pt/MOF–NH_{2}(x)}$ catalysts show almost identical pore size distributions, cantered about ${\sim}2.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ , confirming the existence of mesopores. However, the BET surface area and pore volumes declined further with the addition of Pt as revealed in Table 1, verifying the successful anchoring and encaging of Pt nanoparticles within the mesopores of MOF$\ensuremath{\operatorname{NH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ .
## 3.1.4. SEM and TEM analysis
SEM technique was used to investigate the surface morphology of the as-prepared MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ and ${3{\mathrm{-Pt/MOF-NH_{2}(x)}}}$ catalysts. It is clear that the virgin MOF- $\mathrm{\NH}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ and $3–\mathrm{Pt/MOF–NH_{2}(x)}$ (Fig. 4 & Fig. S4) retains almost a similar crystal morphology and exhibit both even and uneven polyhedron shapes with noticeable corners and edges, concurrence with the reported literature [32,33]. Although, the surface morphology of the Pt nanoparticles supported MOF- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ catalysts was observed to be somewhat rougher (Fig. 4) when compared to pristine MOF- $\mathrm{\NH}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ supports (Fig. S4) due to anchoring of Pt nanoparticles within the mesopores of MOFs through polyol reduction method.
The distribution and structure of Pt nanoparticles in 3-Pt/MOF$\boldsymbol{\mathrm{NH}}2(\mathbf{x})$ catalysts were investigated by TEM, high resolution TEM (HRTEM), and elemental micrographs (Fig. $5\ \&$ Fig. 6). The TEM images (Fig. 5) specify that the small size Pt nanoparticles are homogeneously dispersed and anchored all over the mesoporous MOF- $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ support, encaged within the mesopores of MOF$\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ support and no obvious aggregation of Pt nanoparticles or presence on the external surface were observed. The estimated average sizes (d) of Pt nanoparticles are slightly changed with the alteration of amines precursor’s ratios (Table 1 and Fig. 5), and it suggests that the moderate amount of amines precursors $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ are beneficial to get smaller and more evenly confined Pt nanoparticles within the mesopores of MOFs. Besides, the percent uniform dispersion of Pt nanoparticles was measured from the TEM analysis. The calculated percent dispersion for $3{\cdot}\mathrm{Pt}{}/\$ MOF- $\cdot\ N\mathrm{H}_{2}(2)$ was much higher than other investigated catalysts, as shown in Table 1. Besides, the elemental analysis of 3-Pt/MOF$\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}(2)$ catalyst was executed thru EDX technique and is presented in Fig. S11, where the amount of Pt (weight $\%$ ) determined by ICPOES (Table 1) was also approved by EDX analysis in Fig. S11 (inset). The crystalline structure of the Pt nanoparticles in 3-Pt/MOF$\ensuremath{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}(2)$ and their homogenous distribution was further verified by HRTEM and the resultant elemental micrographs (Fig. 6). The calculated d-spacing of $0.226\ \mathrm{nm}$ from the observed crystal fringes confirms the (111) plane of the face-centered cubic Pt crystals [2,19], which is also in agreement with XRD results (Fig. 1). The elemental micrographs (Fig. 6) reveal the even distribution of Pt nanoparticles. To sum up, the TEM study recommends that the small-size homogenously dispersed Pt nanoparticles encaged within the mesopores of amine-functionalized MOFs (MOF$\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{\eta},}\end{array}$ , can be efficiently synthesized by polyols-reduction method. It is also evident that nitrogen species, together with higher surface area and mesoporous nature of MOF- ${\bf\nabla\cdot N H}_{2}({\bf x})$ supports, are considered favorable for the extra-ordinary homogenous dispersion of active Pt nanoparticles.
## 3.1.5. Surface species analysis
The surface oxidation states of active Pt species in the $3{\cdot}\mathsf{P t}/$ M $\mathrm{IOF-NH}_{2}(0.5)$ , ${3{\mathrm{-Pt}}/{\mathrm{MOF}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}(1)}$ , ${3{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Pt}}/{\mathrm{MOF}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}(2)}$ , 3-Pt/MOF$\mathsf{N H}_{2}(4)$ and the recycled $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF–NH}_{2}(2)$ samples were evaluated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy-XPS. XPS-Survey spectra are displayed in Fig. S5, and the Pt 4f XPS spectra of all the evaluated catalysts were deconvoluted into different surface oxidation states by fitting Gaussian peaks after Shirley-background subtraction (and by fitting the peak separation and area ratios between the
$4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ peaks due to spin–orbit splitting) are presented in Fig. 7. From Fig. 7, an overlap between Pt 4f $(4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ and $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ ) at about $70{\mathrm{-}}76\ \mathrm{eV}.$ The peaks can be deconvoluted to the corresponding Pt 4f regions $(\mathrm{Pt}^{0},\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}\&\ \mathrm{Pt}^{4+})$ . The $\mathsf{P t}^{4+}$ was not observed, and only the reduced Pt species $(\mathrm{Pt}^{0}\&\mathsf{P t}^{2+})$ were observed. The possible reason for the reduction of Pt to $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pt}}^{0}\&\ensuremath\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pt}}^{2+}$ species is the existence of N metal species in the matrix of MOF (confirmed in survey XPS spectra, Fig. S5), which willingly share its lone pair of electrons to improve the surface electron population of the d-orbital of active Pt metal species [30]. The binding energies (B.E) of Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ at
$70.5\substack{-71.5\mathrm{eV}}$ and Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ at $74{-}75~\mathrm{eV}$ could be consigned to the characteristics of the zero-valent metallic Pt nanoparticles [11]. In the same way, the B.E of Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ at $72.5\substack{-73.5\mathrm{~eV}}$ and Pt $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ at $75\mathrm{-}76~\mathrm{eV}$ might be allocated to the characteristics of the oxidized Pt $(\mathsf{P t}^{2+})$ species as mentioned in Fig. 7, and Table S2, which may be the formation of Pt-N or Pt-O bonds on the catalysts surface as illustrated in the literature mentioned above [2,11,30,32]. Moreover, the position of the metallic $\ensuremath{\mathsf{P t}}^{0}$ XPS peak shifted towards lower B.E (red-shift) upon the modification of amines $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right).$ ) functionalized terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ ratios (Table $S2\ \&$ Fig. 7). This suggests the net charge sharing (lone pair of electrons) from N heteroatom to vacant ${\mathsf{d}}\cdot{\mathsf{\Pi}}$ -orbital of active Pt species in $3{\cdot}\mathrm{Pt}{}/$ MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ [30,31]. Hence, the XPS analysis indicates that the active Pt nanoparticles are stabilized and electron-rich due to the intensive interaction of Pt and the N of MOFs support. In addition, the calculated abundance and fractions of each Pt species $({\mathrm{Pt}}^{0}\ \&$ $\mathsf{P t}^{+2}$ ) are stated in Table 1. The content of metallic $\mathsf{P t}^{0}$ enhanced with controlled (moderate) addition of 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ containing N metal species, as for $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF–NH}2(\mathrm{x})$ when compared to non-functionalized Pt catalyst [26]. The one important observation from the XPS analysis is that the surface net charge (electron density) of the active Pt species can be adjusted and improved via the controlled addition of amines (– $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ ) functionalized terephthalic acid.
## 3.1.6. $N H_{3}$ -TPD titration
The Bronsted and Lewis-acid sites on the catalysts (MOF- $\cdot\ N\mathrm{H}_{2}(2)$ and ${3{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Pt}}/{\mathrm{MOF-NH}_{2}(2)})$ surface were identified and measured by ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ -TPD experiments as presented in Fig. S7. The appearance of wide characteristic peaks can be credited to the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ desorption, bonded to the open surface Bronsted and Lewis-acid sites, ratifying the existence of surface Bronsted-Lewis acid sites [11,15,28,29,33]. In quantitative terms, the number of surface acid sites markedly inclined (from $0.45\mathrm{mmol/g}$ on pristine MOF to $0.89\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}$ for 3- $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{M O F-N H}_{2}(2))$ with the addition of active Pt metal species as revealed in Fig. S7. Meanwhile, with the addition of Pt species to MOF- $\cdot\ N\mathrm{H}_{2}(2)$ interior, an increase was observed in the amount of weak Bronsted plus Lewis-Acid Sites $\:140-190\:^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\:\:)$ , moderate Lewis-Acid Sites $200-320^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and strong Lewis-Acid Sites (340 – $400~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ when compared to pristine MOF- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}(2)$ where slightly weak Bronsted plus Lewis-acid sites and mostly moderated Lewisacid sites were identified [4,11,29]. Hence, these analyses indicate the new additional surface adsorption sites generated at active Pt nanoparticles or at the interface between the MOFs support and the Pt nanoparticles.
## 3.2. Catalytic activity results
## 3.2.1. Activity over $3–P t/M O F–N H_{2}(x)$
The selective hydrogenation of $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ , $\upbeta$ -unsaturated aldehydes (CAL and FFL) over mesoporous amine-functionalized MOF supported Pt nanoparticles catalysts $({3}{\mathrm{-Pt/MOF-NH_{2}}}(\mathbf{x}))$ were carried out as a representative reaction, and the results are summarized in Table 2. As described in Table 2, the catalytic performance, more specifically, the selectivity towards $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond hydrogenation, was significantly influenced by the functionalization of MOF with free amines $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ functional group and further variation of amine (– $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ ) functionalized terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ quantity when compared to non-functionalized MOF supported monometallic Pt-based catalysts (no amines $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ in MOF skeleton) [26]. The Pt-based catalyst (no amines $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ in MOF skeleton i.e. $3{\cdot}\mathrm{Pt}{}/$ MOF) has lower conversion $(53.0\ \%)$ as well as lesser selectivity $(20.4\%)$ towards $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond hydrogenation under 1 MPa hydrogen $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ pressure in $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $60\mathsf{C}$ as shown in Table 2. In contrast, the selectivity of COL was markedly improved to $45.4\ \%$ by the introduction of amines $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ functional group to the environment of the catalyst, while the conversion was also enhanced to $57.0\%$ over $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF–NH}_{2}(0.5)$ catalyst in $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ under the same reaction conditions. When the concentration of amines functionalized organic linkers $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ precursors is enhanced in the matrix of MOF$\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ the selectivity towards COL is further enhanced efficiently. As presented in Table 2, the conversion of CAL, as well as selectivity towards UOL (COL) unceasingly improved, and the 3-Pt/MOF$\ensuremath{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}(2)$ catalyst has higher selectivity towards COL $(\geq78.9\ \%$ together with higher conversion $(\geq72.3\ \%)$ in $^\textrm{\scriptsize3h}$ under 1 MPa hydrogen pressure at $60\mathsf{C}$ , which is superior as compared to other catalysts. Beyond this, the catalytic conversion of CAL constantly increased $(\geq77.6\%)$ while the selectivity towards COL was declined $(51.9\ \%)$ over $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF}–\mathrm{NH}_{2}(3)$ . Conversely, both the conversion and selectivity towards COL consciously decreased $(55.7~\&$ $48.0\ \%$ , respectively) over $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF}–\mathrm{NH}_{2}(4)$ as listed in Table 2. The decrease in activity and selectivity may be due to over saturation or excessive amines in the matrix of MOFs that may cover or collapse the active interfacial sites. In other words, as illustrated in XPS analysis, the amount of zero-valent metallic Pt species $(\mathsf{P t}^{0})$ decreases that are the main active species and responsible for the increased catalytic performance. Hence, fewer exposed $\ensuremath{\mathsf{P t}}^{0}$ interfacial active sites result in decreased catalytic performance. In addition, the furfural (FFL) hydrogenation to furfuryl alcohols (FOL) was also examined over all the catalysts (Table 2), which also suggests that the moderate amount of amines functionalized organic ligands will be most suitable to achieve a high yield of unsaturated alcohols (FOL, $287\%$ ). Besides, the $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF}–\mathrm{NH}_{2}(2)$ catalysts were compared with other previously reported monometallic Pt-based catalysts is presented in Table S4.
Keeping in view the results listed in Table 2 and Table S4, the catalytic performance of $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF}–\mathrm{NH}_{2}(2)$ catalyst was much superior compared to other tested and reported catalysts. Hence, the subsequent kinetic parameters were accomplished over it.
## 3.2.2. Effect of reaction time over $3–P t/M O F–N H_{2}(2)$
The hydrogenation reaction of CAL to COL was performed over $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF}–\mathrm{NH}_{2}(2)$ at different time intervals (1 to $5\ \mathrm{h}$ under 1 MPa hydrogen pressure at $60\mathrm{{\sfC}}$ . Initially, the conversion of CAL constantly increased while the selectivity towards COL was almost $80\%$ in the first two hours, as shown in Fig. 8(A). When the reaction time was further extended, the conversion increased smoothly while the selectivity of COL started to decline (declined to $78.9\%$ at $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $69.2\ \%$ at $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ ). After $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the conversion was observed at almost $100\%$ while the selectivity of COL declined to $60.9\%$ . The drop in COL selectivity is hydrogenation of both $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ and $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bonds at the same time that fabricate HCOL as shown in Scheme 1. Prolonging reaction time enhanced the equal opportunity for hydrogen molecules to react with $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ and $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond during the hydrogenation reactions that increase the conversion but decrease selectivity. Also, with lengthening the reaction time, the enlarged concentration of CAL and COL molecules may possibly further hydrogenated and hence, a sharp increase in HCOL is observed. Therefore, moderate reaction time $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ should be chosen to prevent the over hydrogenation and to achieve high selectivity of unsaturated alcohols.
## 3.2.3. Effect of reaction temperature over 3-Pt/MOF- $.N H_{2}(2)$
The CAL hydrogenation to COL was performed at various reaction temperatures (30, 45, 60, $70\ \&\ 80\ \mathrm{C}$ over $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF}–\mathrm{NH}_{2}(2)$ under 1 MPa $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ pressure for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . As expected, the conversion of CAL monotonically enhanced with increased reaction temperature as revealed in Fig. 8(B). Importantly the selectivity of COL initially improved $(\approx80\%)$ with increased reaction temperature (30 to 70 $\mathsf{C}$ ) and then dwindled $(70.5~\%)$ at higher temperatures $(80\mathsf{C})$ . The decline in selectivity is due to hydrogenation of $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond together with $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond to form HCOL.
An additional time-dependent kinetic parameter were executed for the CAL hydrogenation at five different reaction temperatures i.e. 30, 45, 60, 70 and $80~^{\circ}C,$ as shown in Fig. S 9(A). A semilogarithmic plots (Fig. S 9(A)) of the CAL conversion $(\%)$ against reaction time (lnC vs Time (min)) exhibit a good linear relationships $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\cal~R~}^{2}=0.986^{\cdot}$ , an assurance of the first-order kinetic model and is in consistent with the previous literatures [42,43]. Following the reaction rate constants (k), which are plotted in an Arrhenius form (lnK vs $\mathsf{T}^{-1}$ ) in Fig. S 9(B), resulted a value for the activation energy (Ea) of $23\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ at low conversion $(<30~\%)$ of CAL. The determined ‘Ea’ value $(23\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol})$ for the $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF–NH}_{2}(2)$ catalyst is relatively lower than that for the previously reported $\sf P t/B N$ $(35\mathrm{~kJ~mol}^{-1},$ ) [42] and Pt/MIL-101 $(42.8~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ ) [43]. Hence, it can be concluded that the modification of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC precursors in $3–\mathrm{Pt/MOF–NH_{2}(x)}$ can lessen the activation energy (Ea) barrier for the selective hydrogenation reactions of CAL and other $\texttt{\textsf{Q}}\beta$ - unsaturated aldehydes molecules.
## 3.2.4. Effect of hydrogen pressure over $3–P t/M O F–N H_{2}(2)$
To study the effect of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ pressure upon the activity and selectivity of COL, we performed the selective hydrogenation of CAL at 60C for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ under different hydrogen $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ pressure over 3-Pt/MOF${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}(2).$ . As described in Fig. 8(C), the increase in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pressure (from 0.6 to $1.2\ \mathrm{MPa};$ results in the higher conversion of CAL (from 35.5 to $70.1\%$ ). The kinetic parameter for the selective hydrogenation of CAL against different $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pressure were executed and the fitting data followed a linear relationship that confirms the first-order kinetic model $\mathrm{'R}^{2}=0.988\mathrm{?}$ dependence between rate of reaction and $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ pressure as shown in Fig. S10. Similarly, the selectivity of COL also enhanced $(\approx80~\%)$ with the increased $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ pressure (up to $1.0\ \mathrm{MPa},$ but slightly declined $(75.9\ \%)$ at higher $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ pressure $(1.2\ \mathrm{MPa})$ ; because at higher $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ pressure, the hydrogenation of $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond also happens together with $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond that leads to producing HCOL. Hence, $1.0\ \mathrm{MPa}$ was selected to achieve a higher yield of COL.
## 3.2.5. Reusability and recyclability of $3–P t/M O F–N H_{2}(2)$
One of the significant issues with heterogeneous catalytic materials is their durability and reusability. Recycling experiments were performed for the selective hydrogenation of CAL over 3-Pt/MOF$\mathrm{NH}_{2}(2)$ at optimal conditions $60~^{\circ}C,$ 1 MPa $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ ). As shown in Fig. 8(D), no considerable loss in catalytic activity or selectivity was observed after up to four rounds (the conversion declined somewhat in the fourth round). XPS analysis found that the metallic Pt species $(\mathsf{P t}^{0})$ was slightly reduced from 68 to $60\%$ (Table 1). The results indicate that during hydrogenation reactions and washing steps, the minute amount of Pt species was lost $(2.79\ \%)$ when compared to the fresh one $(2.90~\%)$ , which was determined by ICP-OES results (Table 1). Thus, the loss of metallic Pt species $(\mathsf{P t}^{0})$ could be the possible reason for the loss of catalytic activity. Moreover, the decrease in physical parameters (BET surface area reduced from 895 to $821~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and pore volume condensed from 1.12 to $0.98~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , Table 1) may also slightly reduce catalytic performances. Hence, these results suggest that the Pt nanoparticles are the main active species. Their uniform dispersion and stabilization within the mesopores of MOFs may play an energetic role in the selective adsorption and hydrogenation of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bonds.
3.3. Discussion about the influence of MOF- ${\cdot}N H_{2}(x)$ support in catalytic performances
The liquid phase selective hydrogenation reactions of $\texttt{\^a}\beta$ - unsaturated aldehydes-UAL (CAL and FFL) were conducted in the liquid-phase for the optimization of the catalytic performances over amine $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right),$ functionalized MOF supported Pt-species catalysts. As elucidated above, the selectivity of COL and FOL is very low over the non-functionalized amines group 3-Pt/MOF catalysts presented in Table 2. In contrast, the selectivity toward $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond is extensively upgraded to above $80\%$ over the $3–\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{MOF}–\mathrm{NH}_{2}(2)$ catalyst (Table 2). Numerous structural characterization results specify the development of strong interaction (synergistic) effect of mesoporous MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ support, containing N heteroatom species, with active Pt species that markedly enhanced the catalytic performances, as shown in Table 2. The following important factors are accountable for superior catalytic performances based on the above results.
First, the surface oxidation (electronic) states of the active Pt species extremely influenced the selectivity towards the target $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=0$ bond). Numerous studies have shown that the electronrich Pt nanoparticles (metallic Pt or $\mathsf{P t}^{0}$ ) can dissociate more $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules into active hydrogen species and selectively adsorb and hydrogenate the carbonyls $({\mathsf{C}}{=}0)$ bond in UAL. At the same time, repel the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond because of the electron repulsive force of the d-band of Pt species [2,3,38,39,40]. In our case, the surface electron density of Pt species was markedly improved due to the interaction of Pt with N species of the amines functionalized MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ support. The electron lone pair of N species of the amines $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ group in MOF- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ play an energetic role in the modified electron density of the 5d unoccupied orbital of active Pt species as confirmed by XPS and CO-IR results (Fig. 7, Fig. S8 and Table 1) [33,34]. As a result, new interfacial electrophilic (electron-deficient species) and nucleophilic (electron-rich species) active sites are generated [29,36,38]. These electrophilic sites act as Bronsted and Lewis acid sites (confirmed by ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ -TPD analysis in Fig. S7), which are much beneficial for the selective adsorption, polarization, and hydrogenation of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond in a, $\upbeta$ - unsaturated aldehydes [1,4,19,26,37]. As a result, the selectivity towards the desired $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond was higher $(\geq80\%)$ over these more exposed interfacial electrophilic or acid sites and nucleophilic active sites of amines $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ functionalized MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ supported Pt-based catalysts (Table 2).
The Second more influential factor that may assist in achieving high catalytic performance is the homogenous dispersion and stabilization of Pt nanoparticles. Previously, it has been proven that nitrogen (N) heteroatom in a catalytic environment plays a vital role in the standardized dispersion and stabilization of the noble metal nanoparticle to make it more effective catalysts [22,29,30,35,36]. According to the TEM results (Fig. $5\ \&$ Table 1), the small size Pt nanoparticles are well-dispersed in mesoporous amine-functionalized MOF, when compared to nonfunctionalized Pt supported MOF catalysts [26]. Thus, it suggests that the presence of a moderate quantity of amines functionalized terephthalic acid (containing N heteroatom) may help in the Pt nanoparticles uniformly distribution $(\approx60\ \%)$ and results in the tightly anchored ultra-small Pt nanoparticles $(\approx2\mathrm{nm})$ over the amine-functionalized MOFs surface. The more uniform distribution of active Pt nanoparticles within the mesopores of the host (MOF$\mathrm{\NH}_{2}(\mathbf{x}))$ surface may enhance the exposure of more interfacial electrophilic and nucleophilic sites, which is more constructive for the selective adsorption and hydrogenation of desired $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond in CAL and FFL molecules as mentioned in Table 2 [2,3].
The third factor contributing to the improved selectivity toward $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond is the steric or confinement effect produced by the narrow channels/mesopores of MOFs [23,24,26,41]. The TEM analysis (Fig. 5) ratified that $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles are confined within the mesopores of MOF- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathbf{x})$ . The confined Pt nanoparticles may induce the steric hindrance (steric effect) that changes the adsorption geometry of CAL or FFL molecules over the 3-Pt.MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ As shown in Scheme 2, the CAL or FFL molecules adsorbed on the Pt nanoparticles surface perpendicularly via the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond end (situated at the tail-end). At the same time, they have sterically hindered the horizontal or parallel adsorption via the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond end (situated in the centre). Therefore, the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond reaches the catalyst’s interfacial active sites that are selectively hydrogenated. As a result, the selectivity towards the hydrogenation of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond was higher ( $\geq80\%$ 1 over amine-functionalized MOF, as shown in Table 2.
To sum up, due to the mutual synergistic effect of the homogeneously distributed interfacial electrophilic and nucleophilic active sites of Pt and N species together with the steric or confinement effect induced by the mesopores of MOFs encaging active Pt species, the selective catalytic hydrogenation performances were higher over ${3{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{P t}}/{\mathsf{M O F}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}(2)$ as shown in Table 2 and Table S4.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, we have effectively established the amines $(-$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ ) functionalized MOF MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ (where $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{\boldsymbol{\times}}}&{{}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{0.5}}}\end{array}$ , 1, 1.5, 2, 3, & 4) to homogenously anchor the Pt nanoparticles via polyol reduction method with more exposed interfacial electrophilic and nucleophilic sites. It has been demonstrated that the subsequent catalyst has exceptional catalytic performance, selectivity, and stability toward the selective hydrogenation of Carbonyls $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{C}=0)$ bond in unsaturated aldehyde (CAL & FFL), when compared to our previous work (no amines $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ in MOF skeleton) and other reported monometallic $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ -based catalysts. The described synthetic method could also be appropriate for generating other catalytic dynamic structures systems. The existence of N of free amines $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ group in MOFs skeletal plays a pivotal role in the uniform dispersion and stabilization of active Pt nanoparticles within the mesopores of MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ and prevent the Pt nanoparticles from over oxidation and agglomeration. The outstanding selectivity and activity toward $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond $(>80\ \%)$ benefit from the cooperation between the uniformly dispersed active Pt nanoparticles and N of amines $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ group present in the structure of MOF- $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{x}})$ that generate interfacial electrophilic and nucleophilic active sites. Our work delivers a new idea for adjusting the surface properties of free amines $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ functionalized MOFs $(\mathsf{M O F-N H}_{2};$ , and supported active Pt nanoparticles on them as an outstanding selective hydrogenation catalyst. This field could be further prolonged by advancing changing the structural features of the MOFs to make it more selective towards the desired $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond hydrogenation in unsaturated biomass-derived compounds into value-added substances.
